/S /H3
/P 111 0 R
/T (WINDOW SHIFT BRAM's 2-read limit forces an 840-cycle stall loop. So, we can use flip-flops here!  Mapping 1,681 elements to registers reduces shift latency )
/E (WINDOW SHIFT BRAM's 2-read limit forces an 840-cycle stall loop. So, we can use flip-flops here!  Mapping 1,681 elements to registers reduces shift latency )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 111 0 R
/T (PEAK STAGING AND BURST AXI WRITE Conditional in-loop writes introduce non-deterministic bus stalls, breaking pipeline flow. Instead, we can store detected peaks in local BRAM first, then burst-write the entire batch to DDR after the loop finished.)
/E (PEAK STAGING AND BURST AXI WRITE Conditional in-loop writes introduce non-deterministic bus stalls, breaking pipeline flow. Instead, we can store detected peaks in local BRAM first, then burst-write the entire batch to DDR after the loop finished.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 111 0 R
/T (LINE BUFFERS Reading 41 separate rows directly from main memory \(DDR\) forces 41 slow, serial lookups. Caching 40 previous rows in BRAM lets us fetch a full 41-pixel vertical column every cycle while reading only 1 new row from DDR.)
/E (LINE BUFFERS Reading 41 separate rows directly from main memory \(DDR\) forces 41 slow, serial lookups. Caching 40 previous rows in BRAM lets us fetch a full 41-pixel vertical column every cycle while reading only 1 new row from DDR.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
